A1. This isn't raised in the city any more. ~ SALARY
A6. You won't find much chicken in this. ~ BANTAM
A12. These have iron constitutions. ~ ROBOTS
A14. The charm of ancient Egypt. ~ SCARAB
A16. This always fills four squares at least. ~ AREA
A17. A heart breaker. ~ SOB
A19. The king's address. ~ SIRE
A20. When you're in this you're moving. ~ TRANSIT
A23. You find them hanging around women. ~ LOCKETS
A26. Half simple. ~ SIM
A27. It might be a stupid Frenchman. ~ ANE
A28. Every bird sanctuary starts this way. ~ AVI
A29. When you're discouraged, go out and get this. ~ LIT
A30. Don't guess: you can make this otherwise. ~ ELSE
A31. These profits have been disappearing recently. ~ NETS
A33. A person who is always starting something. ~ CREATOR
A35. This is puzzling. ~ CRYPTIC
A38. Not sufficient. ~ NS
A39. Biblical. Isa XV. 1. ~ AR
A40. If you keep your cash in this you needn't worry. ~ RESERVE
A44. These never get into the red. ~ CREDITS
A47. A good mechanic puts this on a building without pay. ~ LIEN
A48. The cry of cell life. ~ RAVE
A49. You should never eat this with olives. ~ PIT
A51. This would cause any business to blow up. ~ TNT
A52. Where Mary's little lamb came from. ~ EWE
A53. Shopkeepers do this to please. ~ AIM
A55. A best stepper. ~ ATHLETE
A57. Build up. ~ ENNOBLE
A59. She left her husband because of Ibsen. ~ NORA
A60. One thing we're glad to see a gangster take. ~ RAP
A62. When this is high the day's half over. ~ NOON
A63. The cause of many a shake-up. ~ TREMOR
A66. What an accident always does. ~ OCCURS
A68. The kind of fellow Diogenes was. ~ SEEKER
A69. You'll find them frozen even in July. ~ ASSETS

D1. More than sorry. ~ SORRIER
D2. Even a bad sailor can stand this on a ship. ~ ABEAM
D3. Sharks have a big interest in this. ~ LOAN
D4. The end of a bat. ~ AT
D5. Receiver's Sale (Good old abbr.) ~ RS
D7. Keep working: you'll find this in like manner. ~ AS
D8. You get no credit for this abbreviation. ~ NC
D9. A job in spite of the depression. ~ TASK
D10. Milton made an angel out of him. ~ ARIEL
D11. The wets like this dry. ~ MARTINI
D12. These eat up the profits in grocery stores. ~ RATS
D13. The kind of bank account you can't draw against. ~ NO
D15. This kind of man doesn't get married. ~ BEST
D17. The frequent killing of the stock market. ~ STEER
D18. This is white in France. ~ BLANC
D21. To put away and forget. ~ SALT
D22. Marked down for the Sheriff. ~ INSOLVENT
D24. A snappy comeback from the Teller. ~ OVERDRAWN
D25. A kind of slicker. ~ CITY
D30. A three-legged holdup. ~ EASEL
D32. A dirt collector. ~ SPADE
D33. This is always tired. ~ CAR
D34. Good old printers' measures. ~ ENS
D36. The beginning of a cycle. ~ TRI
D37. Don't take much stock in these (abbr.) ~ COS
D41. Keen cutters. ~ EDITORS
D42. Ooh! An aweful word. ~ RITE
D43. Stepin. ~ ENTER
D44. To walk on the belly. ~ CREEP
D45. Debutantes come out in expensive gowns, but their fathers are satisfied to come out this way. ~ EVEN
D46. These always have suits on their hands. ~ TAILORS
D49. The kind of leg old maids hope to find under their beds. ~ PANT
D50. A crowd. ~ THREE
D53. The way the worm turns. ~ ABOUT
D54. No room for ladies. ~ MENS
D56. A Washington duck. ~ LAME
D58. The start of a tall story. ~ ONCE
D61. This has A beginning—finish it to suit yourself. ~ AN
D64. All's well when you see this. ~ OK
D65. This is usually about when the boss is dictating letters. ~ RE
D66. What Caesar's dog chewed on. ~ OS
D67. An abbreviated cotton shirt. ~ CS


